===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|kepe}}, {{m|enm|kep}}, from the verb (see above).

====Noun====
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|historical}} The main [[tower]] of a [[castle]] or [[fortress]], located within the castle walls.
#: {{syn|en|donjon}}
# The food or money required to keep someone alive and healthy; one's [[support]], [[maintenance]].
#: {{ux|en|He works as a cobbler's apprentice for his '''keep'''.}}
# The state of being kept; hence, the resulting condition; case.
#: {{ux|en|to be in good '''keep'''}}
# {{lb|en|engineering}} A [[cap]] for holding something, such as a journal box, in place.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The act or office of keeping; custody; guard; care; heed; charge; notice.
#* {{RQ:Malory Le Morte Darthur|VII|XXIII|passage=So Sir Gareth strayned hym so that his olde wounde braste ayen on bledynge; but he was hote and corragyous and toke no '''kepe''', but with his grete forse he strake downe the knyght{{nb...}}.
|translation=So Sir Gareth strained him, so that his old wound brast again a-bleeding; but he was hot and courageous and took no keep, but with his great force he struck down the knight […].}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Shepheardes Calender|December|year=1586
|passage=Pan, thou god of shepherds all, / Which of our tender lambkins takest '''keep'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} That which is kept in charge; a charge.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Shepheardes Calender|July|year=1586
|passage=Often he used of his '''keep''' / A sacrifice to bring.}}
# {{lb|en|euphemism|obsolete}} A [[mistress]] {{gl|the [[other woman]] in an [[extramarital]] relationship, generally including sexual relations}}.
#* {{quote-text|en|date=1923-09-09|author=[[w:George Curzon, 1st Marquess Curzon of Kedleston|Lord Curzon]]|title=[Letter to his wife]|text=My Darling Girl, In London the other day I heard a piece of news which may amuse you. The French girl who shot her so-called Egyptian prince and is going to be tried for murder, is the fancy woman who was the Prince's ''''keep'''<nowiki/>' in Paris during the war {{...}}}}

